//!
//! The wave-123 batch finishes the `0x80090000` bucket at 191/191 by landing the
//! last entry wave 113 had deliberately skipped: the 4-byte branch thunk
//! [`fn_80096700`] (`0x80096700`, `b 0x800967F0`, the notice/effect spawner).
//! The body is a one-call tail-branch wrapper around the already-translated
//! [`fn_800967f0`]; the instruction word `0x480000F0` was read from
//! `work/dol/main.elf` rather than inferred from the thunk export. One body,
//! 4 bytes; the details are in `docs/COVERAGE_WAVE123_STAGE.md`.
//!
//! ## Why it exists
//!
//! A course is not one file: the level geometry lives in
//! `course/course<N>_bgdatL{0,1,2}.bin`, the background units in
//! `BG_unt_<name>{,_hd}.bin`, and the object/section tables in
//! `course/course<N>.bin`. The game keeps the current stage and its three
//! neighbours resident, which is why almost every loop here runs `0..4` (stage)
//! and `0..4` or `0..3` (unit). This module owns the in-memory manager that
//! holds those resources and the parser that fills the stage object.
//!
//! ## Start here
//!
//! [`init_stage_resources`] (`0x80081800`) is the entry point: it records the
//! archive handle, copies the four BG-unit name strings into the manager, loads
//! the three BG layers per stage, and creates the BG-unit models. Read
//! [`create_bg_scene`] before it to see who builds the manager and the scene.
//!
//! ## Data model (what the offsets mean)
//!
//! Two object layouts dominate this file (the BG scene is described at
//! [`create_bg_scene`]). Their layout is *observed* from the stores in the
//! translated functions, not from a symbol table:
//!
//! * **BG manager** (`0x328` bytes, vtable `0x8030FDFC`, built by
//!   [`bg_manager_construct`]): +0x04..+0x33 three BG-layer file handles per
//!   stage (stride `0xC`), +0x34..+0x63 three BG-unit model handles per stage
//!   (stride `0xC`), +0x64..+0xA3 four texture handles per stage (stride
//!   `0x10`), +0xA4..+0xE3 four mesh pointers per stage (stride `0x10`),
//!   +0xE4..+0x2E3 sixteen 0x20-byte BG-unit names (stage stride `0x80`),
//!   +0x2E4 archive handle, +0x2E8..+0x323 four resource handles per stage.
//!   Bytes `+0x324..+0x327` are never written by the constructor.
//! * **Stage object** (`0x3B0` bytes, four of them behind the `r13-0x57E8`
//!   pointer): +0x04 course number, +0x08..+0x40 seven section pointers,
//!   +0x24 object list, +0x40..+0x78 seven section counts, +0x94 object count,
//!   +0x9C..+0xB4 seven per-row counts, +0xB0..+0x3B0 the bucket tables built
//!   by [`parse_course_file`].
//! * **Collision area** (fields through `+0xA2`; `0xA8` is the inferred
//!   aligned size, vtable `0x80310DF0`): +0x04 owner,
//!   +0x1C..+0x28 centre x/y and half width/height, +0x2C shape/type byte,
//!   +0x30/+0x34/+0x38 collision masks, +0x3C optional callback,
//!   +0x50..+0x8C eight pairs of x/y separation accumulators, +0x90/+0x94
//!   last contact point, +0x98/+0x9A/+0x9C `u16` overlap flags, +0x9E
//!   dispatch index, +0x9F/+0xA0 path flags, +0xA1 flags, +0xA2 linked flag,
//!   and +0x10/+0x14 intrusive list links. The layout is *inferred* from the
//!   writers/readers in `0x8008C200..0x8008CA50`; the class is unnamed.
//! * **Stage slot** (`0x34` bytes, vtable `0x80310E30`): +0x04 asynchronous
//!   state (`-1` idle), +0x08/+0x0C loader object, +0x14 source object,
//!   +0x18/+0x19 requested ids, +0x1C pending handle, +0x20/+0x24/+0x28/
//!   +0x2C/+0x30 five resource state words. See [`stage_slot_reset`].
//!
//! ## Dependencies and split
//!
//! * [`crate::platform`] - byte-wise big-endian loads/stores and raw calls.
//! * [`crate::engine::startup::SDA_BASE`] - the `r13` small-data base
//!   (`0x8042F680`), recovered from `__init_registers` (`0x800041C0`).
//! * The two readers that expose a loaded bgdat layer
//!   ([`crate::resource::course::bg_layer_data`],
//!   [`crate::resource::course::bg_layer_u16`]) and the object-id tables
//!   ([`crate::resource::course::object_id_table_entry`], ...) live in
//!   [`crate::resource::course`]; this module only stores and consumes the
//!   handles they read.
//! * Calls into code outside the lane are absolute-address calls with the
//!   original argument registers; none of them was "simplified" away.
//!
//! `MEM1`, `BRRES`, `GX` and `r13` are defined in `docs/GLOSSARY.md`.
//!
//! ## Original range and ownership
